/**
@page errno Error stacks and recovery

@tableofcontents

@section errno_contract Pending errors

Each registered thread has a LIFO stack in a process-wide hash table. The main
thread owns process shutdown; workers publish directly. SET_ERRNO() pushes a
frame and returns the supplied asciichat_error_t. Returning an existing error
propagates it without adding a duplicate. Add a frame only for useful context.

CLEAR_ERRNO() removes one frame. CLEAR_ERRNO_ALL() removes this thread's complete
stack. GET_ERRNO() reads the top, or ASCIICHAT_OK. HAS_ERRNO(&snapshot) copies the
top. HAS_ERRNO_CODE(code) searches all pending frames, not handled history.
ASCIICHAT_OK is never a match. Empty stacks and unknown thread IDs return false.

CLEAR_ERRNO_TID(tid), CLEAR_ERRNO_ALL_TID(tid), and
HAS_ERRNO_CODE_TID(tid, code) operate on a full-width platform thread ID.
Unconditional TID operations target that ID's current registration. For an
inspect-then-clear decision use asciichat_clear_errno_if_top(tid, generation,
error_id); an intervening push, clear, or ID reuse makes it return false.
Remote clearing changes only application error state, never another thread's
native errno. Automatic TLS destructors unregister exited native threads.

@section errno_snapshot Snapshot ownership

Contexts are self-contained values. They contain a bounded inline message and
raw backtrace addresses, not pointers into mutable stack nodes. No snapshot
destroy call is necessary. Copies remain valid after pop, cross-thread clear,
thread exit, or process registry shutdown. Source locations refer to static
compiler strings. Printing symbolizes a private backtrace and frees its symbols.

The context structure and removal of writable TLS globals change the library
ABI. Consumers must rebuild against the new header. Replace direct writes to
asciichat_errno/asciichat_errno_context with SET_ERRNO or its system-error
variant. Replace the old separate cross-thread setter with publication by the
offending thread; there is only one authoritative error registry now.

@section errno_scopes Recovery boundaries

The offending thread owns recovery policy. Libraries record and return errors;
application boundaries choose retry, dismissal, connection teardown, or shutdown.

@code
asciichat_errno_scope_t attempt = asciichat_errno_scope_begin();
asciichat_error_t result = try_connection();
if (result == ERROR_NETWORK_TIMEOUT) {
    asciichat_errno_scope_end(attempt, ASCIICHAT_ERRNO_DISMISSED);
    // Retry; errors from before this attempt remain untouched.
} else if (result != ASCIICHAT_OK) {
    LOG_ERRNO_IF_SET("Connection failed");
    return result;
} else {
    ASSERT_NO_ERRNO_SINCE(attempt);
    asciichat_errno_scope_end(attempt, ASCIICHAT_ERRNO_HANDLED);
}
@endcode

HAS_ERRNO_CODE_SINCE(attempt, code) searches only frames newer than the checkpoint.
Scopes use monotonically assigned IDs and a registration generation, not depth,
so concurrent removals cannot cause unrelated older errors to be consumed.
asciichat_errno_checkpoint() observes a suboperation without starting a new
causal chain; use it for propagation checks inside an existing attempt.
Nested scopes resolve inner attempts independently. Starting an operation also
sets a cause boundary: a new failure cannot cite an unrelated older operation.

ASSERT_NO_ERRNO_SINCE detects successful operations that leave newly created
errors unresolved in debug builds. It does not clear errors. ASSERT_NO_ERRNO
checks the whole current stack. Explicitly resolve known recoverable failures
before declaring success; do not blanket-clear caller errors.

For a fatal worker decision, asciichat_errno_request_exit(code) retains the first
nonzero process error independently of stack cleanup. Request normal application
shutdown after recording the decision. Main returns that code after the mode has
stopped its workers. FATAL remains available for unrecoverable startup failures.
A client authentication failure on the server closes that connection, not every
other client's session.

@section errno_causes Causes, history, and timing

A new frame links to the preceding pending frame inside its operation.
LOG_ERRNO_IF_SET prints the chain from outer context toward the original cause.
SET_ERRNO produces a debug breadcrumb rather than a separate error-level incident
at every propagation layer. Top-level handlers still report failures explicitly.

Every frame has a unique ID, thread generation, captured system/WSA error,
wall-clock timestamp, and monotonic creation time. Pending age and resolution
latency use only the monotonic clock. SET_ERRNO_SYS captures the system error
before evaluating formatting arguments, and macro arguments are evaluated once.

Popped frames become bounded history records with handled, dismissed, thread-exit,
or overflow outcomes. Readers can inspect a brief failure even after successful
recovery. This is a diagnostic history, not a guaranteed-delivery event queue.
Snapshots are non-destructive. History overwrite counters make loss explicit.

@section errno_capacity Capacity and error-path safety

The process reserves 1,024 frame slots, at most 64 frames per thread, at most 256
registered threads, and 128 history records. Messages hold 1,023 bytes plus the
terminator and expose message_truncated. No pending-error allocation is performed
on failure, including out-of-memory failures. Pop zeroes and returns a pool slot;
thread exit returns all its slots; shutdown zeroes stacks and history and releases
the platform TLS key. No message or backtrace-symbol heap allocation remains in
the registry.

Two frame slots are reserved for each thread registration, preserving its root
and newest error even when other threads exhaust the shared pool. At a depth or
shared-pool limit, the second-oldest frame is evicted to preserve root and newest
context. dropped_errors counts eviction and failed registration. A registration
limit/TLS failure records an overflow history event, but cannot create a pending
stack. The original function return code is always preserved.

The registry guard uses platform atomics without recursive diagnostic hooks.
Critical sections only manipulate bounded data; logging, handlers, allocation,
and backtrace symbolization run outside them. A push becomes visible when it
releases the guard. A later synchronized reader sees it unless it has already
been popped; history covers that latter case. This is not a wall-clock latency
guarantee and the API is not async-signal-safe.

@section errno_diagnostics Diagnostics worker

lib/debug/stats.c owns a single native worker for all five modes: server, client,
mirror, discovery-service, and discovery. Independent deadlines allow delayed
state and backtrace requests to coexist. Duplicate requests for the same report
coalesce at the earliest deadline. Shutdown wakes and joins the worker before
diagnostic data is destroyed. Signal handlers only set a request flag.

--errno-stacks [TIME] prints pending error stacks, handled history, error totals,
and error registry statistics. --sync-state [TIME] prints synchronization state
and named registry statistics. Each schedules its own one-shot report after TIME
seconds (immediately when omitted); using both produces two independent reports.
Signal and keyboard diagnostic requests still print both reports.
Debug builds monitor synchronization automatically; release builds start the
worker only when diagnostics are requested. Error recording and searching are
available in every build. Backtraces follow ENABLE_ERRNO_BACKTRACES.

WASM can call the synchronous printers and debug_stats_poll from its event loop;
it does not create a native diagnostics worker.

@section errno_tests Tests

Build the production library tests without Criterion:
@code
cmake --preset default -B build -DBUILD_TESTS=ON
cmake --build build --target test-errno-stacks
ctest --test-dir build -R "^errno-stacks$" --output-on-failure
python tests/standalone/errno/smoke.py build/bin/ascii-chat
@endcode

The native suite exercises root/top search, single evaluation, system/WSA
metadata, safe value snapshots, scoped recovery, history timing/overflow,
thread-ID generations, remote clearing, thread exit, simultaneous writers,
global/per-thread capacity, and interruptible diagnostics shutdown.
Run test-errno-stacks --assert-leak in a debug build to verify that an unresolved
successful-operation boundary exits with ERROR_INVALID_STATE.
*/
